// Unpredictable randomness from the operating system, and, separately, a
// seeded generator you can replay. Written in this language over one
// primitive that asks the kernel for octets. A port of Oro's `random`.
//
// API -- unpredictable, from the OS
// ---------------------------------
//   random.System r = random.system()
//   r.octets(n)                -> bytes, n of them
//   r.hex(n)                   -> str, n octets as 2n lowercase hex digits
//   r.integer(low, high)       -> int in [low, high], both ends included
//   r.fraction()               -> float in [0.0, 1.0)
//   r.permutation(n)           -> List<int>, 0..n-1 in a random order
//   r.uuid4()                  -> str, an RFC 4122 version 4 UUID
//
// API -- reproducible, from a seed
// --------------------------------
//   random.Seeded g = random.seeded(42)
//   g.octets(n) / g.hex(n) / g.integer(low, high) / g.fraction()
//   g.permutation(n) / g.uuid4()
//
// --- two generators, told apart by where the call is -------------------------
//
// "Randomness nobody can predict" and "the same sequence again tomorrow" are
// different operations, so there are two generators and no `seed()`:
//
//   random.system().integer(1, 6)     unpredictable, always; you hold it
//   random.seeded(42).integer(1, 6)   reproducible, always; you hold it
//
// A reader of a call site knows which they are looking at without reading
// anything else, and nothing at a distance can turn one into the other the
// way CPython's global `random.seed(0)` can.
//
// The operations are written once. `Source` below is the protocol both
// generators satisfy -- one method, `octets` -- and every algorithm is
// written against it, so the two differ in that one method and nothing
// else: a difference in behaviour between them cannot be written.
//
// `Seeded` is PCG-XSH-RR 64/32 (Melissa O'Neill's `pcg32`), seeded and
// consumed exactly as Oro's is, so `random.seeded(42)` gives the same
// octets, integers, fractions and UUIDs here as it does in Oro.
//
// --- there are no module-level draws -----------------------------------------
//
// `random.integer(1, 6)` was a function here once, and is not: there is one
// way to draw an unpredictable value and it is through a `System` you hold.
// The reason is the pool. A `System` fetches the kernel's octets a block at
// a time and hands them out, so a program that holds one makes one system
// call per BLOCK octets rather than one per draw; a module-level function
// could not hold the pool, and a module-level function that made a `System`
// per call would be a second spelling of the same draw with none of the
// saving -- the exact trap of a convenience that is quietly 256 times the
// work. One spelling, and it is the one that is fast.
//
// Each `random.system()` is its own pool, so call it once and keep it, as
// `io.stdin()` asks. Holding it is not a performance note: it is how the
// saving happens.
//
// --- what a pool of unused octets is, and is not, safe against ---------------
//
// A pool holds kernel octets that have been fetched and not yet handed out.
// The classic hazard is `fork`: a child inherits the parent's pool and both
// hand out the same octets, which is why arc4random and getrandom track a
// fork generation.
//
// **It cannot happen here, because this language cannot duplicate a
// process.** There is no fork, no exec and no posix_spawn -- `runtime/sys.h`
// has no call that makes a second copy of the address space, and `os` offers
// the command line, the environment and `exit` and nothing else. The only
// second process a program can meet is one it did not start.
//
// The two ways a `System` can be doubled inside the language are both safe:
//
//   - `clone(r)` is shallow (reference §6.4), so the copy shares the one
//     `io.Buffer` and no octet is handed out twice;
//   - a value crosses a thread boundary by being MOVED, never shared
//     (docs/concurrency-decision.md), so a `System` is drawn from by one
//     thread at a time. A thread that wants its own draws makes its own
//     `random.system()`, with its own pool, filled from the kernel.
//
// **The rule, if that ever changes:** a primitive that duplicates the
// process image must not let a `System` made before it be drawn from after
// it. The pool is the thing to drop -- the fix is a new `random.system()` in
// the child, not a repair here.
//
// --- where this differs from Oro, and why ------------------------------------
//
// There is no `choice(xs)` or `shuffle(xs)` yet. Both are generic over the
// element type, and a generic function cannot be called from another module
// yet (`lib.first(xs)` is "`lib` has no function `first`"), nor can a method
// have type parameters, which the seeded forms would need. What they are
// built from is here and is not generic:
//
//   choice:   xs[r.integer(0, xs.size() - 1)]
//   shuffle:  for (int i in r.permutation(xs.size())) { out.push(xs[i]); }
//
// `permutation(n)` is Fisher-Yates over 0..n-1, drawing exactly what Oro's
// `shuffle` draws, so a seeded permutation is the order Oro's seeded shuffle
// would put the same list in, and `xs[g.integer(0, n - 1)]` is Oro's seeded
// `choice`. When generic calls cross modules, `choice` and `shuffle` return
// as functions over these.
//
// The kernel's octets arrive in a `List<int>`, because that is what the one
// primitive fills; `from_kernel` copies them into `bytes` at once, so
// nothing past it sees the difference.
//
// Failures: a range with high below low and a negative count are bugs in the caller and trap, as an index out of range
// does. So does the kernel refusing to supply randomness, which Go's
// crypto/rand also treats as fatal: there is nothing a caller could do with
// the error.
import io;

prim int __entropy(int n, List<int> out);

// How many octets one refill fetches, and so how many draws one system call
// pays for.
//
// **256, because that is the largest block that costs one system call.**
// `rt_entropy` (runtime/rt.c) cuts every request into 256-octet chunks,
// because `getentropy` answers no more than 256 per call and that is the one
// spelling glibc, musl, macOS and the BSDs all have. So a 4 KiB pool would
// make sixteen system calls to fill and would save nothing per octet over
// this one; it would only keep fifteen more blocks of unused entropy in
// memory. The optimum is the chunk size itself, and it is 256.
const int BLOCK = 256;

/// Where octets come from: the protocol the two generators satisfy, and the
/// only thing they differ in.
///
/// Private, because it is not a way to write a third generator -- a program
/// that wants one writes its own type with its own methods. It is here so
/// that every algorithm below is written once, against `octets`, and
/// dispatches to whichever generator it was handed. Interfaces are
/// structural (reference §3.4), so neither `Seeded` nor `System` mentions
/// it; each simply has the method.
interface Source {
    bytes octets(int n);
}

/// A reproducible generator: the same seed gives the same sequence, on every
/// platform and every release, because every step of it is integer
/// arithmetic written here. Made by `random.seeded(seed)`.
///
/// Its one field is private, so the only way to make one is `seeded`, and
/// the only way to move it is a draw. (`state` is a list only so that a draw
/// through the `Source` protocol advances the generator it came from.)
pub type Seeded {
    List<int> state;
}

/// n octets.
///
/// This is `Seeded`'s half of the `Source` protocol: the one method the two
/// generators do not share an implementation of.
pub bytes Seeded.octets(int n) {
    check_count(n, "octets");
    return pcg_fill(state, n);
}

/// n octets as 2n lowercase hex digits.
pub str Seeded.hex(int n) {
    return hex_from(this, n);
}

/// A uniform integer in [low, high], both ends included.
pub int Seeded.integer(int low, int high) {
    return integer_from(this, low, high);
}

/// A float in [0.0, 1.0).
pub float Seeded.fraction() {
    return fraction_from(this);
}

/// 0..n-1 in a uniformly random order.
pub List<int> Seeded.permutation(int n) {
    return permutation_from(this, n);
}

/// A version 4 UUID by construction but not in spirit: anyone who knows the
/// seed can predict it. It belongs in a test fixture, never in a database.
pub str Seeded.uuid4() {
    return uuid4_from(this);
}

/// A generator seeded with `seed`.
///
/// The seed is required, so a generator seeded from somewhere the caller did
/// not choose cannot be written. A negative seed is its 64-bit two's
/// complement pattern, a seed like any other. The start is the standard
/// `pcg32_srandom_r` -- state zero, step, add the seed, step again -- so
/// that adjacent seeds do not open with correlated output.
///
/// A function and not `random.Seeded(seed)`, which is what Oro writes: the
/// generator's state is not the seed, and a construction can only store
/// what it is given.
pub Seeded seeded(int seed) {
    return Seeded([step(step(0).wrapping_add(seed))]);
}

/// The system generator: octets nobody can predict, from the operating
/// system, a block at a time. Made by `random.system()`.
///
/// Its draws cost one system call per `BLOCK` octets, not one per draw,
/// which is the whole reason it is an object rather than a set of functions.
/// See the header for what a pool of unused octets is safe against.
pub type System {
    // Fetched from the kernel and not yet handed out. An `io.Buffer` is
    // exactly this thing and is already written (lib/io.src): reading from
    // the front is amortised O(1), and a pool drained to empty reuses its
    // own storage, so a long run of draws neither copies nor allocates.
    io.Buffer pool;
}

/// n octets.
///
/// This is `System`'s half of the `Source` protocol. Anything already in the
/// pool is handed out first, and the pool is refilled from the kernel when
/// it runs short.
pub bytes System.octets(int n) {
    check_count(n, "octets");
    // `io.Buffer.read(0)` traps -- an empty result has exactly one meaning
    // there -- so a draw of nothing is answered before the pool is asked.
    if (n == 0) {
        bytes empty = [];
        return empty;
    }
    // A draw of a whole block or more has nothing to gain from the pool: the
    // kernel is asked for the same number of octets either way, and routing
    // them through the pool would copy them a second time and evict what is
    // there. It goes straight to the kernel and leaves the pool alone.
    if (n >= BLOCK) {
        return from_kernel(n);
    }
    if (pool.size() < n) {
        // One block is always enough, whatever little was left: `n` is below
        // `BLOCK`, so the pool holds at least `n` afterwards.
        Result<int, io.Error> put = pool.write(from_kernel(BLOCK));
    }
    match (pool.read(n)) {
        case Ok(bytes b): {
            return b;
        }
        case Err(io.Error e): {
            // Unreachable: an `io.Buffer` has no kernel to say no to it, and
            // its results are always `Ok` (lib/io.src). Written out because
            // the arm exists, not because it can be taken.
            trap("random: the octet pool refused a read (" + e.to_str() + ")");
        }
    }
}

/// n octets as 2n lowercase hex digits. The count is of octets, not
/// characters: `hex(16)` is 128 bits however it is written.
pub str System.hex(int n) {
    return hex_from(this, n);
}

/// A uniform integer in [low, high], both ends included -- the bound a caller
/// almost always means (a die is `integer(1, 6)`), and the one that can name
/// the whole range of `int`.
pub int System.integer(int low, int high) {
    return integer_from(this, low, high);
}

/// A float in [0.0, 1.0), with all 53 bits of a double's mantissa.
pub float System.fraction() {
    return fraction_from(this);
}

/// 0..n-1 in a uniformly random order: a shuffle of any list of n
/// elements, by index. See the header for why it is not `shuffle(xs)`.
pub List<int> System.permutation(int n) {
    return permutation_from(this, n);
}

/// An RFC 4122 version 4 UUID.
pub str System.uuid4() {
    return uuid4_from(this);
}

/// A generator whose octets come from the operating system, with a pool of
/// its own.
///
/// **Call it once and keep it.** Two of them are two pools, each fetching
/// blocks the other never sees -- the same warning `io.stdin()` carries, for
/// the same reason. A program that makes one per draw is correct and slow,
/// which is why there is no shorter spelling that does it for you.
pub System system() {
    return System(io.buffer());
}

// n octets straight from the kernel, no pool involved.
bytes from_kernel(int n) {
    List<int> got = [];
    int e = __entropy(n, got);
    if (e != 0) {
        trap("random: the operating system supplied no randomness (errno " + e.to_str() + ")");
    }
    bytes out = [];
    for (int b in got) {
        out.push(b);
    }
    return out;
}

// --- the algorithms, once ---------------------------------------------------
str hex_from(Source s, int n) {
    check_count(n, "hex");
    return s.octets(n).hex();
}

// By rejection sampling, never `% span`. A byte modulo 3 maps 256 values
// onto 3, and 256 is not a multiple of 3, so one outcome is 2% rarer than
// the others -- from one line that looks obviously correct. Instead: keep
// only the low bits that cover the span, which is uniform because every bit
// pattern is equally likely, and redraw anything past the end. The redraw
// costs at most one extra draw on average.
//
// Keeping the low `bits` bits is exactly the mask Oro applies; the octets are
// read most significant first, as Oro reads them, so a seeded generator
// answers the same integers in both.
int integer_from(Source s, int low, int high) {
    if (high < low) {
        trap("random.integer(" + low.to_str() + ", " + high.to_str() + "): the range is empty -- high is below low, and both ends are included");
    }
    if (low == high) {
        return low;
    }
    // A span wider than 2^63 does not fit an int; it takes its own path.
    if (low < 0 && high > max_int() + low) {
        return integer_wide(s, low, high);
    }
    int last = high - low;
    int bits = bit_length(last);
    int count = (bits + 7) / 8;
    int drawn = low_bits(s.octets(count), bits);
    while (drawn > last) {
        drawn = low_bits(s.octets(count), bits);
    }
    return low + drawn;
}

// [low, high] spanning more than 2^63 values: all 64 bits are drawn, read as
// an unsigned number `top * 2^63 + rest`, and kept when low plus it is at
// most high -- which accepts more than half of all draws.
int integer_wide(Source s, int low, int high) {
    while (true) {
        bytes b = s.octets(8);
        int top = b[0] >> 7;
        int rest = low_bits(b, 63);
        if (top == 0) {
            // low < 0 and rest < 2^63, and high - low exceeds 2^63 - 1, so
            // this is always inside the range.
            return low + rest;
        }
        // low + 2^63, written so that no step overflows: low is negative.
        int base = low + max_int() + 1;
        if (rest <= high - base) {
            return base + rest;
        }
    }
}

// Seven octets, three bits dropped: 53 bits over 2^53, which is CPython's
// construction and the only one that cannot round up to exactly 1.0.
float fraction_from(Source s) {
    int n = low_bits(s.octets(7), 56) >> 3;
    return float(n) / 9007199254740992.0;
}

// Fisher-Yates over 0..n-1, downwards, which is its own direction: element
// `i` is settled by swapping it with one chosen from 0..i, so every
// permutation is reachable exactly once.
List<int> permutation_from(Source s, int n) {
    check_count(n, "permutation");
    List<int> out = [];
    int k = 0;
    while (k < n) {
        out.push(k);
        k = k + 1;
    }
    int i = n - 1;
    while (i > 0) {
        int j = integer_from(s, 0, i);
        int held = out[i];
        out[i] = out[j];
        out[j] = held;
        i = i - 1;
    }
    return out;
}

// 122 random bits, with the version nibble (4) and the two-bit variant (10)
// written over the draw where RFC 4122 puts them. The hyphens are part of the
// format: this string is the UUID.
str uuid4_from(Source s) {
    bytes b = s.octets(16);
    b[6] = b[6] & 0x0f | 0x40;
    b[8] = b[8] & 0b0011_1111 | 0b1000_0000;
    str h = b.hex();
    return h.substr(0, 8) + "-" + h.substr(8, 12) + "-" + h.substr(12, 16) + "-" + h.substr(16, 20) + "-" + h.substr(20, 32);
}

// --- PCG-XSH-RR 64/32 ---------------------------------------------------------
//
// The state advances as a 64-bit LCG, state * 6364136223846793005 + inc,
// modulo 2^64; the output is the OLD state xor-shifted down to 32 bits and
// rotated by its own top five bits. With the reference demo's arguments (seed
// 42, stream 54) this produces PCG's published output, a15c02b7 7b47f409
// ba1d3330 ...
//
// The state is an `int` holding the 64-bit pattern, so the LCG is the two
// wrapping methods and the output function is bit operators. `>>` copies the
// sign bit in, and PCG means the unsigned shift, so every right shift of the
// state goes through `shr`.
//
// step: state * multiplier + increment, mod 2^64. The increment is Oro's stream
// constant, shifted left once with its low bit set:
// (1442695040888963407 << 1) | 1.
int step(int state) {
    return state.wrapping_mul(6364136223846793005).wrapping_add(2885390081777926815);
}

// One 32-bit output: the previous state is what gets permuted, so the
// multiply and the output function are independent of each other.
int next32(List<int> state) {
    int old = state[0];
    state[0] = step(old);
    int xorshifted = shr(shr(old, 18) ^ old, 27) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    int rot = shr(old, 59);
    // Rotate right by rot within 32 bits. A shift by 32 is in range, so
    // rot == 0 needs no case of its own; the mask drops what it pushes up.
    return (xorshifted >> rot | xorshifted << 32 - rot) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
}

// n octets: each 32-bit output gives four, most significant first, and what
// is left of the last one is dropped -- Oro's order, octet for octet.
//
// The dropped remainder is why a `Seeded` has no pool and must not get one:
// keeping those octets for the next draw would be free and would change
// every sequence, and the sequences are Oro's.
bytes pcg_fill(List<int> state, int n) {
    bytes out = [];
    while (out.size() < n) {
        int v = next32(state);
        int shift = 24;
        while (shift >= 0 && out.size() < n) {
            out.push(v >> shift & 255);
            shift = shift - 8;
        }
    }
    return out;
}

// The logical right shift, `x >>> n` for 1 <= n <= 63: the arithmetic shift
// with the copied-in sign bits masked off, as the reference spells it (§6.1).
int shr(int x, int n) {
    return x >> n & ~(-1 << 64 - n);
}

// --- small arithmetic -------------------------------------------------------
int max_int() {
    return 0x7FFF_FFFF_FFFF_FFFF;
}

// How many bits it takes to write `n`, which is positive here.
int bit_length(int n) {
    int bits = 0;
    int rest = n;
    while (rest > 0) {
        bits = bits + 1;
        rest = rest >> 1;
    }
    return bits;
}

// The octets as one number, most significant first, keeping only the low
// `bits` bits (1..63). The top octet is masked before anything is shifted
// past it, so eight octets never reach the sign bit.
int low_bits(bytes b, int bits) {
    int n = b.size();
    int top = bits - 8 * (n - 1);
    int out = b[0] & (1 << top) - 1;
    int i = 1;
    while (i < n) {
        out = out << 8 | b[i];
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return out;
}

void check_count(int n, str who) {
    if (n < 0) {
        trap("random." + who + "(" + n.to_str() + "): a count cannot be negative");
    }
}
